/* Sample spoken answers: polished scripts a strong candidate could say, per step.
   Bold marks phrases worth reusing. open = playback + clarifying questions. */
window.CASE_SCRIPTS = {
diapers:{
 open:"<p>So, <b>to make sure I understand</b>: our client is an international hygiene products company with no presence in Saudi Arabia, considering a local factory. <b>The first question is</b> how big the KSA diaper market is. A few quick questions: should I include adult diapers as well as baby diapers? Disposable only? And should I size it <b>in dollars per year</b>? <b>Could I take a moment to structure my approach?</b></p>",
 steps:{
  "Approach":"<p><b>I'll size this top-down, by age.</b> Start with the population, and divide it by life expectancy to get the number of people at each age. Diapers are used by two groups: babies up to about three, and some elderly people, so I'll focus on those. For each group, I'll estimate the share that uses diapers and how many they use per day. That gives diapers per day, then per year. Finally I'll multiply by the price per diaper to get the market in dollars. <b>Does that approach sound reasonable?</b></p>",
  "Population":"<p><b>I'll assume</b> a population of 35 million and a life expectancy of 80. <b>To keep the math simple</b>, I'll assume people are spread evenly across ages, so 35 million divided by 80 is about 437,000. <b>I'll round that to 450,000 people per year of age.</b></p>",
  "Diapers per day":"<p><b>Let me go group by group.</b> Ages 0 to 1: 450,000 babies, all using diapers, 10 a day, so 4.5 million. Ages 1 to 2: 90% use them, about 405,000 children at 8 a day, so roughly 3.2 million. Ages 2 to 3: 80%, about 360,000 at 6 a day, so about 2.2 million. For 75 to 80, <b>that's five years of age, so 2.25 million people</b>; 30% use diapers, about 675,000 people at 4 a day, so 2.7 million. <b>Adding it up, that's about 12.6 million diapers a day.</b></p>",
  "Market value":"<p>12.6 million a day times 365 is about <b>4.6 billion diapers a year</b>. A pack of 12 at $6 is 50 cents a diaper. So 4.6 billion times 50 cents gives a market of <b>about $2.3 billion a year</b>.</p>",
  "Sanity check":"<p><b>It feels reasonable, and if anything conservative.</b> Babies drive about 80% of the volume, so those assumptions matter most. I assumed an even age distribution, but <b>Saudi Arabia has a young population</b>, so there are probably more babies than I assumed, which would push the number up. On the other side, prices vary between premium and discount brands. <b>So I'd call $2.3 billion a conservative estimate.</b></p>",
  "The factory decision":"<p>At 10% share, the client would have <b>$230 million in revenue</b>. At a 15% margin, that's about <b>$34.5 million of profit a year</b>. $100 million divided by $34.5 million is a <b>payback of about 2.9 years</b>. <b>One caveat:</b> the $2.3 billion is at store prices. The client sells to retailers, who keep perhaps 25 to 30%, so real revenue is closer to $160 to 170 million and <b>payback is nearer four years</b>. That's still attractive.</p>",
  "What else to check":"<p><b>I'd group what I'd check into three areas.</b> First, <b>competition</b>: who leads today and how loyal customers are to their brands, which tells us if 10% is realistic. Second, <b>go-to-market</b>: access to pharmacies, hypermarkets and online channels. Third, <b>the operating environment</b>: local manufacturing incentives, Saudization requirements, and demand trends like the birth rate. <b>A lower-risk option</b> could be to import first and build once share is proven.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>I recommend moving ahead with the factory, subject to two checks.</b> First, the market is large: about $2.3 billion a year, likely more given the young population. Second, the economics work: at 10% share, the $100 million factory pays back in three to four years even after retailer margins. <b>The key risk</b> is whether a new entrant can really reach 10%. <b>As next steps</b>, I'd validate that against current brands' positions and secure distribution partners and any local incentives.</p>"
 }},
hotdog:{
 open:"<p><b>Just to play it back</b>: our client owns one hot dog stand in Hamra. It was profitable for years, profits have dropped over the last few months, and this month it lost money. <b>The goal is to find out why and fix it.</b> A few questions: what do they sell, and at what price? Has the price changed? Is the competition seeing the same decline? And is there a profit target we're aiming for?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd look at this in two parts.</b> First, <b>revenue</b>: price times the number of meals sold, and whether either has changed. Second, <b>costs</b>: food, rent, staff, utilities, and any waste. <b>To find the cause, I'd compare this year with last year line by line.</b> And since Frank's is doing fine, <b>my hypothesis is that the problem is specific to our stand</b> rather than the market.</p>",
  "Last year vs. this year":"<p><b>Last year:</b> 20 meals at $6 for 30 days is $3,600 of revenue. Food at $2 a meal is $1,200, and the other costs add up to $1,700, so a profit of <b>$700 a month</b>. <b>This year:</b> 15 meals gives $2,700, food is $900, other costs $1,900, so <b>a loss of $100</b>. <b>So profit fell by $800.</b> Lost revenue explains $900 of it, partly offset by $300 less food bought; waste added $150 and utilities $50. <b>The main problem is volume: we sell a quarter fewer meals.</b></p>",
  "Two clues":"<p><b>The two are connected.</b> The owner is probably still buying food for 20 meals a day while selling 15, so more of it expires. On volume, <b>I'd split the possible causes into internal and external.</b> Internal: quality, service speed, opening hours. External: a competitor's move, less foot traffic, the economy. <b>Since Frank's is doing fine, I'd want to know why our customers left.</b> Do we have any customer feedback?</p>",
  "Customer survey":"<p><b>The biggest driver is value</b>: 45% switched for Frank's $5 student combo, which includes fries. 25% mention our wait times, which is <b>something we can fix cheaply</b>. Together, <b>70% of the reasons are about value and service, not quality or location.</b> <b>So what:</b> the market didn't change, the competitor did, and we didn't respond.</p>",
  "Should they cut the price?":"<p>At $5, we keep $3 per meal instead of $4. Monthly costs to cover are 800 plus 500 plus 400 plus 50, so $1,750. <b>Breakeven is 1,750 divided by 3, about 583 meals a month, or about 19 to 20 a day.</b> <b>That means winning back almost every lost customer just to break even.</b> By comparison, keeping $6 and fixing the waste breaks even at about 15 a day. <b>So I wouldn't cut the price; I'd match Frank's on value instead.</b></p>",
  "What should they do?":"<p><b>I'd act on three fronts.</b> First, <b>the quick win</b>: order based on actual demand, saving about $150 a month. Second, <b>compete on value, not price</b>: a $6 combo with fries or a student deal. Third, <b>fix service</b>: prepare ahead and add help at lunch peak. Then win students back with a loyalty card. <b>Each extra meal a day is worth about $120 a month</b>, so small gains add up quickly.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>The stand is losing about $100 a month because it lost five meals a day to Frank's new student combo, and kept over-ordering food.</b> I recommend three actions. One, <b>order for actual demand</b>, which recovers about $150 a month immediately. Two, <b>launch a value combo at $6 rather than cutting the price</b>, which would push breakeven up to 20 meals a day. Three, <b>speed up lunch service.</b> <b>As a next step</b>, I'd track daily sales and waste for a month to measure progress.</p>"
 }},
concert:{
 open:"<p><b>To recap</b>: our client is a Saudi events company offered a one-night concert in Riyadh with a famous DJ, and they need to decide within two weeks <b>whether it's worth doing</b>. A few questions: <b>how do they define success</b>, is there a profit target? What's the venue capacity? And do we have data from their past events?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd look at three areas.</b> First, <b>profitability</b>: revenue from tickets by tier, sponsors and extras, minus fixed costs like the DJ and venue, and per-ticket costs like commissions. Second, <b>demand</b>: how many tickets will realistically sell. Third, <b>feasibility</b>: the license, local rules and cultural fit. <b>I'd then compare the expected profit with their $10,000 threshold.</b></p>",
  "Full-house revenue":"<p>VIP is 400 tickets at $50, so $20,000. Regular is 600 at $20, so $12,000. Sponsors add $3,000, $2,000 and $1,000, so $6,000. <b>Total revenue at a full house is $38,000.</b></p>",
  "Full-house profit":"<p><b>I'll split costs into fixed and variable.</b> Fixed: DJ $6,000, venue $5,000, maintenance and insurance $4,000, operations $4,200, so $19,200. Variable: $3 commission on 1,000 tickets is $3,000. Total costs $22,200, <b>so profit is $15,800</b>, <b>comfortably above the $10,000 bar if it sells out.</b></p>",
  "Realistic sales":"<p><b>Using past sell-through:</b> 70% of VIP is 280 tickets, $14,000; 90% of regular is 540 tickets, $10,800. Sponsors stay at $6,000, so revenue is $30,800. Costs are $19,200 fixed plus $3 on 820 tickets, $2,460, so $21,660. <b>Expected profit is about $9,100, roughly $860 short of the target.</b> <b>So the full-house case is misleading.</b></p>",
  "Closing the gap":"<p><b>The gap is small, under $1,000, so there are several ways to close it.</b> On revenue: food and drink stands, merchandise, parking, or one more sponsor. For example, <b>500 people spending $5 at a 50% margin is $1,250</b>, which alone closes it. On tickets: an early-bird presale to lift VIP sales. On costs: negotiate the commission or the venue fee.</p>",
  "Beyond the numbers":"<p><b>I'd think about risks in three groups.</b> <b>Regulatory</b>: getting the license on time. <b>Operational</b>: crowd safety, and the DJ's cancellation terms. <b>Commercial</b>: whether the format fits the audience and local norms, and whether there's enough time to sell tickets.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>I recommend going ahead, but only once extra income is secured.</b> A full house makes $15,800, but based on past events the realistic profit is about $9,100, just under their $10,000 minimum. <b>Securing one food concession or one more sponsor closes that gap.</b> <b>Next steps:</b> lock in the license and the DJ's cancellation terms, and start presales early.</p>"
 }},
banker:{
 open:"<p><b>Let me make sure I have it</b>: our client made their fortune in real estate, has up to $25 million, and is considering entering Saudi banking. <b>The questions are whether to do it, and how.</b> Could I ask why banking? What's their return expectation or timeline? And do they have any banking experience?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd structure this in four parts.</b> First, <b>market attractiveness</b>: size, growth and profitability of Saudi banking. Second, <b>competition and barriers</b>: how concentrated the market is, licensing, Saudization. Third, <b>client fit</b>: no experience, and a goal of diversifying. Fourth, <b>entry options and financials</b>: building a bank versus buying one, and payback against their 10-year goal.</p>",
  "The market":"<p><b>Two takeaways.</b> The market is attractive: profits are growing about 6% a year. But it's <b>highly concentrated</b>, with the top five holding 75% of deposits, so winning share from scratch is hard. <b>The opportunity is in faster-growing niches</b> like digital banking and small-business lending. And with two years to license and launch, <b>this points toward buying a foothold rather than building.</b></p>",
  "Build or buy":"<p><b>Option 1</b> earns 22% of $10 million, $2.2 million a year, so 15 divided by 2.2 is about 6.8 years of profit. <b>But it earns nothing for the first two years</b>, so real payback is <b>about 8.8 years</b>. <b>Option 2</b> is $20 million for $2.5 million a year, <b>8 years</b>, starting immediately, with 120,000 existing customers and Saudization already handled. <b>So buying is both faster and lower risk.</b></p>",
  "The catch":"<p><b>Yes, one significant concern.</b> 35% of the target's loans are to real estate. <b>The client's goal was to diversify away from real estate</b>, so this would double down on it: a property downturn would hit their core wealth and the bank at the same time. <b>I'd make the deal conditional on due diligence of that loan book</b>, and a plan to reduce the real-estate share over time.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>I recommend entering Saudi banking by acquiring the existing bank, with one condition.</b> Three reasons: it pays back in 8 years versus almost 9 for a new bank; it comes with customers and Saudization compliance; and it avoids the risk of building with no experience. <b>The condition</b> is reducing the 35% real-estate exposure, since that works against the diversification goal. <b>Next steps:</b> loan-book due diligence, a valuation check, and retaining the management team.</p>"
 }},
tv:{
 open:"<p><b>So, to recap</b>: a Dubai family business with restaurants and a bus company is considering buying a TV channel for $15 million, and <b>wants to earn it back in under five years.</b> A couple of questions: how does the channel make money? Do they have media experience, or will management stay? And why do they want this channel?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd look at four areas.</b> <b>Financials</b>: revenue, costs and payback against five years. <b>Market</b>: competitors and advertising trends. <b>Client fit</b>: expertise and management. And <b>synergies</b>: whether the restaurants and buses benefit. <b>Since the five-year payback is the deal-breaker, I'd start with financials.</b></p>",
  "Maximum ad revenue":"<p><b>Let me do peak and off-peak separately.</b> Peak: $250 a minute, 10 minutes an hour, 4 hours, 300 days, so <b>$3 million</b>. Off-peak: $100, 10 minutes, 20 hours, 300 days, so <b>$6 million</b>. <b>Maximum revenue is $9 million a year.</b></p>",
  "Payback":"<p>Revenue of $9 million minus $6 million in costs is <b>$3 million profit</b>. $15 million divided by $3 million is <b>exactly five years</b>. <b>That just misses their goal of under five.</b></p>",
  "Reality check":"<p><b>Not quite. $9 million assumes every ad slot sells, so it's a ceiling.</b> With actual revenue of $8 million, profit is $2 million, and <b>payback is 7.5 years, well beyond the goal.</b> <b>Interestingly</b>, peak is sold out, so peak prices may be too low, while off-peak has about $1 million of unsold slots.</p>",
  "Synergies":"<p><b>That's a meaningful synergy.</b> The family could move about $1 million of its ad spend into the unsold off-peak slots. <b>It's not new money for the family; it's a saving</b>, but at group level it brings effective profit back to about $3 million and <b>payback to five years: still not under five.</b> I'd also check whether off-peak slots suit restaurant ads.</p>",
  "The right price":"<p><b>At $2 million of profit a year, five years of profit is $10 million.</b> So they shouldn't pay more than about $10 million. <b>I'd keep the synergies as negotiating room</b>, not as the base case.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>I recommend not buying at $15 million.</b> Three reasons: real payback is 7.5 years, not five; the $9 million figure is a ceiling the channel doesn't reach; and even moving the family's own ads only gets payback to five years. <b>I'd counter-offer around $10 million.</b> <b>Next steps:</b> test the seller's flexibility on price, and look at raising peak ad prices.</p>"
 }},
plane:{
 open:"<p><b>To make sure I understand</b>: our client is launching a daily Dubai–Paris route on a 300-seat plane, and <b>wants the ticket price that maximizes profit per flight.</b> Questions: is it a single cabin class? Do we know how demand changes with price? And what are the costs per flight?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Approach":"<p><b>My approach</b>: first, find how seats sold change with price, a demand curve. Then <b>write profit as a function of price</b>: revenue is price times seats, minus fixed costs and per-passenger costs. <b>Then find the price where profit peaks</b>, and sanity-check it against competitors.</p>",
  "Demand":"<p>Going from $1,000 to $2,000 loses 100 seats, so the slope is minus 0.1 seats per dollar. Plugging in $1,000 and 200 seats gives an intercept of 300. <b>So seats equal minus 0.1 times price, plus 300.</b> Checking: at $2,000, that gives 100. ✓</p>",
  "Best price":"<p>Revenue is P times (300 minus 0.1P), which is <b>minus 0.1P squared plus 300P</b>. Costs are $10,000 plus $500 times seats, which is <b>160,000 minus 50P</b>. So profit is <b>minus 0.1P squared plus 350P minus 160,000</b>. It peaks where minus 0.2P plus 350 equals zero, so <b>P equals $1,750</b>. That sells 125 seats, for about <b>$146,000 profit per flight</b>.</p>",
  "Sanity check":"<p><b>It makes me cautious.</b> We'd be the most expensive airline on the route, with fewer daily flights than either competitor. Our demand curve comes from research; once customers compare fares, demand might be lower. <b>And we'd fill only 125 of 300 seats, 42%</b>, which is very low for an airline. <b>So the math is right, but the result is fragile.</b></p>",
  "The empty seats":"<p><b>Yes.</b> 75 seats at $900 with a $500 cost each is <b>$30,000 more profit per flight</b>. <b>The risk is cannibalization</b>: full-fare travelers switching to cheap seats. <b>I'd manage that with restrictions</b>: group bookings only, fixed dates, no refunds. That's how airlines use fare classes.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>I recommend a $1,750 headline fare, plus restricted discount fares to fill empty seats.</b> The headline fare maximizes single-price profit at about $146,000 per flight, and a group fare adds about $30,000 more. <b>The main risk</b> is that we're priced above both competitors, with a low load factor. <b>Next steps:</b> test real demand at launch, and design fare rules that prevent cannibalization.</p>"
 }},
energy:{
 open:"<p><b>Let me recap</b>: the Ministry wants to <b>cut national energy consumption by 10% within five years</b>. Consumption grows about 5% a year, faster than the population, and energy is heavily subsidized. A few questions: is the 10% against today's level? Are there political constraints on raising prices? And do we have data on which sectors use the most?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd build the program in three steps.</b> First, <b>where energy goes</b>: homes, commercial buildings, industry and transport. Second, <b>the levers</b> for each: price, efficiency and technology, regulation, and behavior. Third, <b>feasibility</b>: cost, political acceptability, and impact. <b>I'd start by finding the biggest consumers</b>, since that's where the 10% must come from.</p>",
  "Where the energy goes":"<p><b>Residential is the biggest sector at 40%</b>, and air conditioning is 70% of that. <b>So home AC alone is about 28% of national consumption</b>, the single biggest target. <b>I'd start there.</b></p>",
  "Size one lever":"<p>40% times 70% times 30% of homes times 30% savings is <b>about 2.5% of national consumption</b>. <b>So what:</b> one ambitious program delivers only a quarter of the target. <b>We'll need a package of measures, not a single lever.</b></p>",
  "Homes and businesses":"<p><b>I'd group ideas into three types.</b> <b>Standards</b>: minimum efficiency for new AC units and appliances, and building codes for insulation and shading. <b>Technology</b>: smart thermostats, occupancy sensors, district cooling in new developments. <b>Behavior</b>: smart meters that show usage, and awareness campaigns.</p>",
  "Industry and transport":"<p><b>For industry</b>: energy audits, incentives for efficient equipment, and higher prices for the heaviest users. <b>For transport</b>: fuel-efficiency standards for new cars, better public transport, car-pooling, electric vehicle charging, and <b>gradually reducing fuel subsidies</b>.</p>",
  "The politics":"<p><b>I'd make price changes gradual and targeted.</b> Use <b>tiered pricing</b>: the first block of usage stays cheap, so ordinary households are protected, while heavy users pay more. Phase increases in over several years with clear communication. Support low-income families directly. <b>Then recycle the subsidy savings</b> into rebates for efficient air conditioners.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>Minister, I recommend a package, not a single measure, because no single lever gets us to 10%.</b> <b>First</b>, target home air conditioning, which is 28% of all energy, with efficiency standards and rebates. <b>Second</b>, introduce gradual tiered pricing that protects ordinary households. <b>Third</b>, efficiency programs for industry and transport. <b>Next steps:</b> size each lever so the package adds up to 10%, and pilot in one city first.</p>"
 }},
bank:{
 open:"<p><b>To recap</b>: our client is a large Saudi retail bank whose <b>revenue fell from $1 billion to $900 million over two years</b>, while the market grew slightly, <b>so they're losing share</b>. The CEO wants to know why and what to do. Questions: how is revenue split across products? How have competitors done? And has anything changed in regulation?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd split this into external and internal factors.</b> External: the economy, interest rates, regulation, and new competitors. Internal: <b>which product lines declined</b>, and for each, whether it's <b>fewer customers or less revenue per customer</b>. <b>Since the market grew while we shrank, I'd start internally.</b></p>",
  "Which category?":"<p><b>Let me convert the shares to dollars.</b> Personal loans and cards went from 55% of $1 billion, $550 million, to 50% of $900 million, $450 million: <b>down $100 million</b>. Car loans went from $280 to $270 million, down $10 million. House loans went from $170 to $180 million, up $10 million. <b>So personal loans and cards explain the entire decline.</b> Note that car loans' share rose, but in dollars they actually fell.</p>",
  "Customers or price?":"<p><b>It's customers.</b> We lost 100,000 customers, about 17%, while revenue per customer barely moved, from about $917 to $900. <b>So this is a churn problem, not a pricing problem.</b> The next question is where those customers went.</p>",
  "Why are they leaving?":"<p><b>To summarize where we are</b>: revenue fell $100 million, all in personal loans and cards, because we lost 100,000 customers. <b>The survey explains why</b>: half moved to digital banks for instant approval and a better app, and 30% left over card fees. <b>So the root cause is our digital experience</b>, which links to the digital banks that entered two years ago.</p>",
  "Growing again":"<p><b>Selling more to existing customers is easier and cheaper</b>: we already have the relationship and their data, and customers rarely switch banks. <b>The main lever is cross-selling</b>: credit cards to loan customers, and loans to card customers. In parallel, I'd run <b>retention offers</b> for customers at risk of leaving.</p>",
  "Size the cross-sell":"<p>30% of 500,000 have only a loan, so 150,000. 10% take a card, so <b>15,000 new cardholders</b>. Annual fees: 15,000 times $250 is <b>$3.75 million</b>. Default charges: 5% default, 750 customers, times $20,000 of spending times 10%, is <b>$1.5 million</b>. <b>Total: $5.25 million a year.</b></p>",
  "Is it enough?":"<p>Adding $18.75 million from the other direction, <b>cross-selling brings about $24 million</b>. <b>That's only about a quarter of the $100 million we lost.</b> So it helps, but it doesn't solve the problem: <b>we also have to stop customers leaving for digital banks.</b></p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>Revenue fell $100 million because we lost 100,000 personal loan and card customers, mostly to digital banks.</b> I recommend two moves. <b>Short term</b>, cross-sell cards and loans to existing customers, worth about $24 million a year. <b>Long term</b>, fix the root cause: a better app, instant approvals, and a review of card fees. <b>Next steps:</b> identify customers at risk of leaving, and decide whether to build or partner on digital.</p>"
 }},
crypto:{
 open:"<p><b>Let me play that back</b>: our client is a Lebanese fintech startup that wants to launch a cryptocurrency for everyday payments, at a time when trust in banks is low. <b>The question is whether it's a good idea.</b> A few questions: what does success look like for them? Is it legal today? And who are the main competitors?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd look at four areas.</b> <b>Market</b>: how many people use crypto in Lebanon, and why. <b>Competition</b>: Bitcoin's 60% share and how hard it is to get people to switch. <b>Profitability</b>: fees times transactions, minus costs, and payback against their three-year goal. And <b>risks</b>: regulation, trust and volatility.</p>",
  "How many users?":"<p>Six million people spread over four age groups is 1.5 million each. Crypto users: 10% of the youngest is 150,000; 25% of 21 to 40 is 375,000; 5% of 41 to 60 is 75,000; and none over 60. <b>That's 600,000 crypto users.</b> Bitcoin has 60%, so the other 40% is about <b>240,000 potential users. That's a ceiling, not a forecast.</b></p>",
  "Transactions":"<p>High income: 10% of 240,000 is 24,000, at 7 a week, so 168,000. Middle: 120,000 at 3, so 360,000. Low: 96,000 at 1, so 96,000. <b>About 624,000 transactions a week.</b></p>",
  "Profit":"<p>The fixed charge is 50 cents times 624,000, which is <b>$312,000</b>. The average commission is half at 60 cents, 40% at 40 cents and 10% at 20 cents, so <b>48 cents</b>, which gives about <b>$300,000</b>. Revenue is about $612,000 a week, and at a 10% margin, <b>profit is about $61,000 a week</b>.</p>",
  "Payback":"<p>$61,000 a week times 52 is about <b>$3.2 million a year</b>. $5 million divided by $3.2 million is <b>about 1.6 years: comfortably within their three-year goal</b>, if we reach those users.</p>",
  "Stress test":"<p><b>That's the key question.</b> Profit per user is about $3.2 million divided by 240,000, roughly <b>$13 a year</b>. To recover $5 million in three years, we need about $1.7 million a year, so <b>about 126,000 users, more than half the non-Bitcoin market.</b> <b>That's ambitious for a new coin.</b> The thin 10% margin also matters, because servers, licensing and marketing are expensive.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>I recommend launching, but as a staged pilot.</b> The upside is real: about 1.6-year payback if adoption is strong. <b>But the case depends on winning about 126,000 users</b>, more than half the market Bitcoin doesn't hold, and the rules are still unclear. <b>Next steps:</b> pilot with merchants to test adoption, engage the central bank early, and measure users' willingness to switch.</p>"
 }},
hair:{
 open:"<p><b>So, to recap</b>: our client has a one-pill permanent cure for hair loss, just approved in Lebanon. <b>The CEO wants a first-year revenue estimate and a view on whether the launch pays off.</b> Questions: is it really one pill per person? Are there competing treatments, and what do they cost? And what did development cost?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>Revenue is price times buyers, so I'd structure it that way.</b> <b>Price</b>: the value to the customer, alternatives like transplants, and our costs. <b>Buyers</b>: who has hair loss, who can afford it, and how fast they adopt. <b>Then, for the payoff question</b>, development and running costs.</p>",
  "Price":"<p><b>I'd anchor on the alternative.</b> A transplant costs $5,000 and involves surgery. Our pill is one dose, permanent and simple, so it deserves a premium. <b>I'd price it at 1.5 to 2 times a transplant, $7,500 to $10,000</b>, starting at the lower end because incomes in Lebanon limit what most people can pay.</p>",
  "Who wants it":"<p><b>Men:</b> 20% of 750,000 aged 21 to 40 is 150,000; 50% of 41 to 60 is 375,000; 40% of 61 to 80 is 300,000. That's 825,000. <b>Women:</b> 5% of 41 to 60 is 37,500, and 20% of 61 to 80 is 150,000, so 187,500. <b>About 1 million people are interested.</b></p>",
  "Who can afford it":"<p><b>No, income matters at $7,500.</b> High income: 10% of 1 million, all able to pay, so 100,000. Middle: 400,000 times 30%, so 120,000. Low: none. <b>So 220,000 people can afford it.</b></p>",
  "Year-one revenue":"<p><b>Not all in year one</b>: new products are adopted gradually, starting with early adopters. At 20%, that's <b>44,000 buyers times $7,500, about $330 million in year one.</b></p>",
  "Will it pay off?":"<p>Manufacturing is 44,000 times $100, $4.4 million, plus $40 million of marketing, so <b>year-one profit is about $286 million</b>. <b>That recovers about 70% of the $400 million R&D in the first year.</b> <b>But there's a catch</b>: each person buys only once, so the Lebanese market shrinks after early adopters. <b>The real payoff comes from launching in other countries.</b></p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>I recommend launching at around $7,500.</b> First-year revenue is about $330 million and profit about $286 million, so R&D is recovered within about two years. <b>The main risks</b> are that the price premium may not hold if results match a transplant, and that a one-time purchase means each market dries up. <b>Next steps:</b> plan the international rollout, test price sensitivity, and consider financing to reach middle-income buyers.</p>"
 }}
};
